;;; `dead` is NOT upward-closed under `absent`, which is why a refutation may
;;; not rest on an `absent` a commitment can still fill (Q-M1e2.5).
;;;
;;; ── The premise this refutes ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; An early design of the search read:
;;;
;;;   dead(X) — X holds a contradiction. Monotone: X ⊆ Y ∧ dead(X) ⇒ dead(Y),
;;;   because the KB is append-only and nothing retracts.
;;;
;;; *Append-only, nothing retracts* makes saturation **inflationary**. It does
;;; not make it **monotone in its input**, and `(absent P)` is exactly what
;;; separates the two — which `docs/ein/reasoning/02-kb-evolution/03-absent-and-naf.md`
;;; § What follows says from the other direction: removing a fact can flip an
;;; absent and *fabricate* a contradiction the full KB never had.
;;;
;;; Here `bad` fires only while `q` is missing:
;;;
;;;   alive₀          = {(p A), (q A)}
;;;   {(p A)}         is DEAD   — bad fires, (q A) absent
;;;   {(p A), (q A)}  is ALIVE  — the guard's witness is present
;;;
;;; so the single maximal state is {(p A), (q A)} and dead is not upward-
;;; closed. Three mechanisms of a search that read the false premise, each
;;; sufficient alone, answered wrongly on it: a lookahead kill cache (writing
;;; `(not (p A))` with no premises), a singleton writeback, and a width-1
;;; no-good clause (subsuming the live superset, so it was never generated).
;;; Five of that search's six configurations answered wrongly, and every one
;;; of them reported `exhausted = true`.
;;;
;;; ── What this file is ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A refusal's fixture: `bad` concludes `(false)` from `(absent (q ?x))`
;;; over `q`, which a commitment can still extend, so the program is refused
;;; at load (*forbid*, Q-M1e2.5). Every question about a commitment (`ein
;;; solve` in every mode, `ein test`, `ein oracle`, `ein render lattice`)
;;; exits 1 with one line naming `bad`, `(false)`, `q`, and the forms that
;;; say what was meant; `ein saturate` still saturates. Regression
;;; naf-upward-closure.
;;;
;;; Loaded anyway, its R would be three states, `∅`, `{(q A)}` and
;;; `{(p A), (q A)}`, with the dead hole `{(p A)}`: what *respect* would
;;; answer, kept as the row's alternative and as the library-level fixture
;;; the read-out's dead-hole line is held on (`forbid::studying`).
;;;
;;; The goal is `is-a` rather than `p`. An answer with an empty goal extent
;;; is a claim like any other, `(model)` the class of a state where the goal
;;; has no row (Q-M1e.13); the goal stays as it was written.
;;;
;;; ── A mis-encoded obligation ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The user's reading:
;;;
;;; > not any well-formed ein program is correct. An example with
;;; > `(rule bad () …)` is broken by design … there is no definition of `q`
;;; > and `p` relations, no obligations, only the rule that asserts `(false)`.
;;;
;;; It says *a world with p and without q is false* and never says *q is
;;; required*, which is what `(open ?R)` is for (Q-M1d.2). The engine's
;;; complaint is not wrong; the word is. That reading is why the refusal
;;; names `(open ?R)` as one of its replacements — beside `total`'s
;;; stored-negative discipline, and closing the relation where no commitment
;;; is meant to extend it.
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;

(relation is-a T T :why "{?3} is a {?4}")
(relation p T :why "{?3} has p")
(relation q T :why "{?3} has q")

(is-a A T)

(rule bad ()
  :match  (and (p ?x) (absent (q ?x)))
  :assert (false)
  :why    "{?x} has p without q")

(query
  :goal   (is-a ?x T))
